QUESTIONS.push(
/* ---------------- Definitions ---------------- */
{id:"L13-001", lecture:"L13", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L13", sub:"defs", skill:"tell",
 concept:"toxin-vs-toxicant", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Coral snake venom and the fumes from a factory smokestack are, in the terms toxicologists use, respectively a:",
 options:[
  {t:"Toxin and a toxicant", correct:true, why:"A toxin is a toxic substance produced by a biological system (snake venom); a toxicant is produced by, or is a by-product of, human activity (smokestack emissions)."},
  {t:"Toxicant and a toxin", correct:false, why:"This swaps the two: biological origin makes the venom a toxin; the man-made emission is the toxicant."},
  {t:"Xenobiotic and a poison", correct:false, why:"Both words apply loosely to both substances; the pair that separates biological from anthropogenic origin is toxin versus toxicant."},
  {t:"Toxin and a toxin", correct:false, why:"Smokestack emissions are not made by a biological system."},
  {t:"Poison and a xenobiotic", correct:false, why:"Poison is the general term for anything that causes a harmful response; it does not say where the substance came from."}],
 teach:"Poison is the general lay term for any agent that can cause a deleterious response in a biological system. Toxin and toxicant both name noxious substances, but a toxin comes from a biological system (venom) and a toxicant from human activity (industrial emissions). A xenobiotic is any compound in an organism that the organism does not normally make or expect: it may be a toxin, a toxicant, a food additive or a medication.",
 quote:"toxins are substances coming from biological systems, so. Snake venom is a toxin, OK? ... A toxicant is made by man through our activities.",
 note:"She added: \"I will often not use those words properly, OK? That's OK. I just wanted you guys to know that ... those two words are out there and they're different.\"",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Definitions’; transcript 10/9"},

{id:"L13-002", lecture:"L13", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L13", sub:"defs", skill:"recall",
 concept:"xenobiotic", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Which statement about a xenobiotic is correct?",
 options:[
  {t:"A compound not normally present in or produced by the organism", correct:true, why:"That is the definition; a xenobiotic may be a toxin, a toxicant, a food additive or a medication."},
  {t:"It is always poisonous", correct:false, why:"A xenobiotic is defined by being foreign to the organism, not by being harmful; a medication is a xenobiotic."},
  {t:"It is only a man-made chemical", correct:false, why:"That describes a toxicant; a xenobiotic can also be a natural toxin."},
  {t:"A substance produced only by a biological system, such as a venom", correct:false, why:"That describes a toxin; a xenobiotic can be man-made."},
  {t:"It is a drug that has already been metabolized", correct:false, why:"Metabolism is not part of the definition; the parent compound is a xenobiotic from the moment it enters the organism."}],
 teach:"Xenobiotic is the broad word for any compound that gets into an organism and is not normally produced by or expected in it. Food additives, medications, toxins and toxicants all qualify.",
 quote:"It just means any sort of compound that gets into an organism that's not normally produced by that organism or expected to be present in that organism",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Definitions’; transcript 10/9"},

/* ---------------- The five principles ---------------- */
{id:"L13-003", lecture:"L13", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L13", sub:"princ", skill:"recall", multi:true,
 concept:"five-principles", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Which of the following are among the five principles of toxicology? Select all that apply.",
 options:[
  {t:"Source", correct:true, why:"Where the poison comes from: environmental, occupational, bites and stings, medications."},
  {t:"Exposure", correct:true, why:"Route, duration, single versus repeated, and frequency."},
  {t:"ADMET", correct:true, why:"Absorption, distribution, metabolism, excretion, with a T added for toxicity; the metabolism part includes bioactivation."},
  {t:"Dose", correct:true, why:"The dose makes the poison; dose–response curves give LD50, threshold and the safety indices."},
  {t:"Target and mechanism of action", correct:true, why:"Where in the body the substance acts and how it causes harm."},
  {t:"Therapeutic index", correct:false, why:"The therapeutic index is a value calculated within the dose principle, not one of the five principles."},
  {t:"Route of administration", correct:false, why:"Route is one factor within the exposure principle."}],
 teach:"Toxicologists organize any poisoning around five principles: the source, the exposure (route, duration, single versus repeated, frequency), ADMET (absorption, distribution, metabolism, excretion, toxicity), the dose, and the target with its mechanism of action.",
 quote:"We think about the source of a toxin or toxicant. We think about how are people or critters exposed. We have to think about ADM ... we're gonna add the T ... Another principle is dose ... And then the last principle is we think about target and mechanism of action",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘5 Principles of Toxicology’; transcript 10/9"},

{id:"L13-004", lecture:"L13", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L13", sub:"princ", skill:"recall",
 concept:"admet-t", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"In the toxicology version of ADME, written ADMET, the added T stands for:",
 options:[
  {t:"Toxicity", correct:true, why:"Absorption, distribution, metabolism, excretion, and toxicity."},
  {t:"Target", correct:false, why:"Target and mechanism of action is a separate principle."},
  {t:"Threshold", correct:false, why:"Threshold belongs to the dose–response discussion."},
  {t:"Transport", correct:false, why:"Transport is part of distribution, not a separate letter."},
  {t:"Time", correct:false, why:"Duration and frequency belong to the exposure principle."}],
 teach:"The kinetics of a poison follow absorption, distribution, metabolism and excretion; toxicologists add a T for toxicity, because the whole point is what the compound does at its target once it is there.",
 quote:"We're in the toxicology module here, so we're gonna add the T to add me, OK? So absorption, distribution, metabolism, elimination, and toxicity",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘5 Principles of Toxicology’; transcript 10/9"},

{id:"L13-005", lecture:"L13", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L13", sub:"princ", skill:"apply",
 concept:"toxic-target-differs", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"A medication's toxic effect and its therapeutic effect:",
 options:[
  {t:"May act at different targets", correct:true, why:"The target of a toxic effect is not always the therapeutic target; acetaminophen is used for pain and fever, but its toxicity is in the liver."},
  {t:"Always act at the same receptor", correct:false, why:"Toxicity can come from a different organ or molecule than the one the drug was designed to act on."},
  {t:"Always occur in the same organ", correct:false, why:"The organ of toxicity can differ from the organ of benefit."},
  {t:"Are distinguished only by dose", correct:false, why:"Dose matters, but the target can differ as well."},
  {t:"Are never related to metabolism", correct:false, why:"Metabolism (bioactivation) can create the toxic species."}],
 teach:"Within the target-and-mechanism principle, the place where a substance causes harm may be a different organ or molecule from its therapeutic site. Dose at the target, not the dose given, is what determines toxicity.",
 quote:"sometimes the target of something toxic is not the same target as its therapeutic target, OK? We'll talk about that a little bit more later.",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Target & Mechanism of Action’; transcript 10/9"},

/* ---------------- Source ---------------- */
{id:"L13-006", lecture:"L13", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L13", sub:"source", skill:"apply",
 concept:"source-categories", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"A pharmacist is exposed to chemotherapy while mixing it in a hood with the wrong airflow. Which source category does this exposure belong to?",
 options:[
  {t:"Occupational", correct:true, why:"A workplace exposure; healthcare workers can be exposed to chemotherapy, radiation, or a contaminant on a patient brought to the emergency room."},
  {t:"Environmental", correct:false, why:"Environmental exposure is pollution in the surroundings, such as diesel fumes from a passing bus or dust from a landfill."},
  {t:"Medication, as a patient", correct:false, why:"The pharmacist is not taking the drug; the exposure comes from the job."},
  {t:"Critter bite or sting", correct:false, why:"No animal is involved."},
  {t:"Pathogen exposure", correct:false, why:"Toxicology deals with chemicals; needle sticks and pathogens are not what this principle covers."}],
 teach:"The four source categories are environmental exposures (pollution, contaminated dust), occupational exposures (the workplace, including chemotherapy, radiation and contaminated patients for healthcare workers), critter bites and stings, and medications.",
 quote:"do you have a risk of occupational exposures as a healthcare provider? ... you could be exposed to um chemotherapy, you know, uh, mixing it up in a in in an in an incorrect hood with the wrong airflow ... Remember, in this course we're not usually thinking about exposure to pathogens like needle sticks",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Source’; transcript 10/9"},

/* ---------------- Exposure: route ---------------- */
{id:"L13-007", lecture:"L13", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L13", sub:"route", skill:"recall",
 concept:"route-effectiveness-order", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Ranked from most to least effective for delivering a toxic agent, the routes of exposure are:",
 options:[
  {t:"Intravenous, inhalation, oral, dermal", correct:true, why:"The descending order of effectiveness on the slide; intravenous delivery puts the whole dose into the blood at once."},
  {t:"Oral, intravenous, inhalation, dermal", correct:false, why:"Oral is the most common route of exposure, not the most effective."},
  {t:"Inhalation, intravenous, dermal, oral", correct:false, why:"Intravenous ranks first and dermal last."},
  {t:"Dermal, oral, inhalation, intravenous", correct:false, why:"This is the reverse order."},
  {t:"Intravenous, oral, dermal, inhalation", correct:false, why:"Inhalation ranks second, above oral."}],
 teach:"The major routes of exposure are oral (the most common), inhalation, topical and parenteral; healthcare sees more parenteral exposure than other settings. In descending order of effectiveness they run intravenous, inhalation, oral, dermal.",
 quote:"IV is gonna be your most effective route. If you can give it by IV, if it's a liquid and you can inject it, IV is the most effective route, and then you can see inhalation, oral, and dermal, uh, they decrease in, in the effectiveness",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Exposure: Route’; transcript 10/9"},

{id:"L13-008", lecture:"L13", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L13", sub:"route", skill:"apply",
 concept:"route-first-pass-toxicity", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"A compound is metabolized by the liver to a less toxic product. At equal doses, which route of exposure is the less dangerous one?",
 options:[
  {t:"Oral: first pass removes part of the dose", correct:true, why:"An oral dose passes through the liver first; if the liver makes a less toxic product, the oral route protects."},
  {t:"Intravenous: the liver clears it faster", correct:false, why:"An intravenous dose skips first-pass metabolism, so the whole dose reaches the body in its toxic form."},
  {t:"The routes are equivalent at equal doses", correct:false, why:"Route changes toxicity whenever first-pass metabolism changes the compound."},
  {t:"Intravenous: it bypasses the stomach and gut wall", correct:false, why:"Bypassing the stomach is not protective here; bypassing the liver is what makes the intravenous route worse."},
  {t:"Dermal: nothing crosses the skin", correct:false, why:"The comparison in the question is between oral and intravenous exposure to the same dose."}],
 teach:"Route affects toxicity when the same compound can enter by more than one route. An oral dose undergoes first-pass metabolism in the liver; if the liver converts the compound into something less toxic, the oral route is the least scary one, while an intravenous dose avoids that protection.",
 quote:"If you had equivalent doses, oral versus IV, and this compound goes through first pass effect. Which is the least scary route of administration. The oral is right, it'll help protect you",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Exposure: Route’; transcript 10/9"},

{id:"L13-009", lecture:"L13", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L13", sub:"route", skill:"apply",
 concept:"formulation-overdose", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"A patient swallows a handful of extended-release β-blocker tablets. Which statement about the formulation is correct?",
 options:[
  {t:"Extended-release tablets can clump and then release all at once", correct:true, why:"Her answer to immediate- versus extended-release was \"it depends\": immediate release absorbs quickly; extended-release tablets can clump and then dump the drug."},
  {t:"Extended-release tablets are always the safer of the two overdoses", correct:false, why:"They are not; the delayed, clumped release can be as dangerous as a fast one."},
  {t:"Immediate-release tablets are always the safer overdose", correct:false, why:"Immediate release gives rapid absorption; neither form is always safer."},
  {t:"The formulation does not change toxicity", correct:false, why:"Formulation and vehicle are two of the factors that change the toxic response to a route of exposure."},
  {t:"Only the vehicle, not the release form, matters", correct:false, why:"Both the vehicle (oily versus aqueous) and the formulation matter."}],
 teach:"Vehicle and formulation change the toxicity of an exposure. An oily vehicle behaves differently from an aqueous one, and immediate-release and extended-release forms of the same drug differ in overdose: immediate release absorbs quickly, while extended-release tablets can clump in the stomach and release later all at once.",
 quote:"If somebody were to take a handful of an immediate release beta blocker, compared to taking a handful of extended release beta blockers, which do you think is worse? It depends ... the extended release. Oftentimes those compounds, they will, those tablets will clump together in a person's stomach. And then all of a sudden it gets released",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Exposure: Route’; transcript 10/9"},

/* ---------------- Exposure: duration, single vs repeated ---------------- */
{id:"L13-010", lecture:"L13", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L13", sub:"duration", skill:"recall", type:"match",
 concept:"exposure-durations", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Match each duration of exposure used in animal studies with its definition.",
 left:["Acute", "Subacute", "Subchronic", "Chronic"],
 right:["Less than 24 hours", "Less than 1 month", "1 to 3 months", "More than 3 months, up to years"],
 pairs:[
  {l:"Acute", r:"Less than 24 hours", why:"A single day or less."},
  {l:"Subacute", r:"Less than 1 month", why:"Repeated exposure for up to a month."},
  {l:"Subchronic", r:"1 to 3 months", why:"One to three months of repeated exposure."},
  {l:"Chronic", r:"More than 3 months, up to years", why:"Three months or longer."}],
 teach:"Animal exposure studies define the duration of exposure: acute is less than 24 hours, subacute less than one month, subchronic one to three months, and chronic more than three months up to years. Humans are exposed on the same scales, but a person often cannot say how long a workplace or environmental exposure has been going on.",
 quote:"acute is for exposure for less than 24 hours. For for animal studies, for subacute, it's less than 1 month. Sub-chronic is 1 to 3 months, and then chronic is more than 3 months up to years",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Exposure: Duration’; transcript 10/9"},

{id:"L13-011", lecture:"L13", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L13", sub:"duration", skill:"apply",
 concept:"single-vs-repeated-benzene", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"A worker who walks into a building with a small benzene leak gets a headache and feels sleepy; a worker exposed to the same leak every day for years develops leukemia. This illustrates that:",
 options:[
  {t:"A single exposure and repeated exposure can differ in effect", correct:true, why:"Benzene is her example: one exposure gives headache and drowsiness; chronic low-dose exposure is carcinogenic."},
  {t:"Tolerance to benzene develops with repeated workplace exposure", correct:false, why:"The daily worker still gets the headache every day; no tolerance develops."},
  {t:"The route of exposure changed", correct:false, why:"Both workers inhaled the same gas."},
  {t:"Benzene has a long half-life", correct:false, why:"Half-life was not the point of this example; the point is that the kind of effect changes with repetition."},
  {t:"Leukemia is an immediate effect of benzene", correct:false, why:"Leukemia is the delayed effect of chronic exposure."}],
 teach:"The first of three points about single versus repeated exposure: the effect of one exposure can be very different from the effect of repeated exposure. A single benzene exposure causes headache and sleepiness; chronic low-dose exposure causes leukemia.",
 quote:"anybody know what the dangerous. Health effect. Uh, that can occur with benzene exposure, chronic low dose benzene exposure ... it's a carcinogen, huh, causes leukemias, uh, so wow, getting sleepy and a headache is really different than, you know, like getting leukemia",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Exposure: Single vs. Repeated’; transcript 10/9"},

{id:"L13-012", lecture:"L13", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L13", sub:"duration", skill:"apply",
 concept:"delayed-effect-des", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"Women given diethylstilbestrol (DES) during pregnancy felt little more than nausea, but their daughters developed an unusual vaginal cancer about twenty years later. This is an example of:",
 options:[
  {t:"A delayed effect of an exposure", correct:true, why:"The exposure was in utero; the effect appeared about twenty years later, which took epidemiology to uncover."},
  {t:"An immediate effect of a fast-absorbing compound", correct:false, why:"Fast-absorbing compounds give immediate effects; DES is her example of the opposite, a delayed effect."},
  {t:"Tolerance to a hormone", correct:false, why:"No tolerance is involved."},
  {t:"A hormetic response", correct:false, why:"Hormesis is a beneficial low-dose effect; this is a harmful delayed one."},
  {t:"A route-dependent toxicity", correct:false, why:"Route is not what the example teaches."}],
 teach:"The second point about exposure: fast-absorbing compounds usually produce immediate effects, but some exposures produce a delayed effect. DES, an estrogen used in the 1950s to help women keep a pregnancy, caused a specific vaginal cancer in the daughters exposed in utero, about twenty years after the exposure.",
 quote:"We started seeing a very weird and scary phenomenon about 20 years after these women were given DES ... the female children of these women ... would develop a very certain kind of vaginal cancer",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Exposure: Single vs. Repeated’; transcript 10/9"},

{id:"L13-013", lecture:"L13", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L13", sub:"duration", skill:"recall",
 concept:"chronic-immediate-each-time", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"The worker exposed to the unrepaired benzene leak feels sleepy with a headache every single day he goes in. Which point about exposure does this show?",
 options:[
  {t:"A chronic exposure can also give an immediate effect each time", correct:true, why:"The third point on the slide: repeated exposure does not stop each exposure from producing its immediate effect; no tolerance developed."},
  {t:"A chronic exposure produces only delayed effects, never immediate ones", correct:false, why:"The immediate effect keeps happening with every exposure."},
  {t:"Tolerance removes the immediate effect", correct:false, why:"In this example no tolerance developed."},
  {t:"The exposure must have been a single one", correct:false, why:"It is daily, so chronic."},
  {t:"Half-life determines the headache", correct:false, why:"Half-life is the frequency discussion, not this one."}],
 teach:"The third point about single versus repeated exposure: a chronic exposure can still produce an immediate effect after each exposure. The benzene worker gets the headache and sleepiness every day while the leak goes on, with no tolerance.",
 quote:"chronic exposure may produce immediate effects after each exposure ... Every time I walk into my workplace, I just get sleepy and a little bit of a headache ... they're not developing any tolerance",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Exposure: Single vs. Repeated’; transcript 10/9"},

/* ---------------- Exposure: frequency and half-life ---------------- */
{id:"L13-014", lecture:"L13", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L13", sub:"freq", skill:"figure",
 concept:"fig2-4-halflife", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"In the figure of compounds A, B and C given as one large single dose, the concentration of A declines most slowly, B faster and C fastest. Which compound has the longest half-life?",
 options:[
  {t:"A", correct:true, why:"The slowest fall in blood concentration means the longest time for half of the compound to be cleared."},
  {t:"B", correct:false, why:"B clears faster than A; its half-life is about equal to the dosing frequency in the repeated-dose panel."},
  {t:"C", correct:false, why:"C clears fastest, so it has the shortest half-life."},
  {t:"All three have the same half-life", correct:false, why:"The slopes differ."},
  {t:"It cannot be read from a single-dose curve", correct:false, why:"The rate of decline after one dose is exactly what shows the half-life."}],
 teach:"Casarett and Doull Figure 2-4 gives three compounds one single dose and plots blood concentration against time. A, with a half-life of about a year on the figure caption, stays high; B has a half-life about equal to the dosing interval (about a day); C, with a half-life of about five hours, clears quickly. A toxic response occurs above the shaded concentration.",
 quote:"Which compound has the longest half-life in this image? ... A, so it's half-life is longest, right? It takes longer for half the compound to be cleared out of the blood, OK? C has the shortest half-life.",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Exposure: Frequency’ (Figure 2-4 and its caption); transcript 10/9"},

{id:"L13-015", lecture:"L13", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L13", sub:"freq", skill:"figure",
 concept:"fig2-4-repeated", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"The same three compounds are then given repeatedly, each dose one quarter of the single dose, at the same interval. Which compound never reaches the toxic concentration?",
 options:[
  {t:"C: its short half-life clears each dose before the next", correct:true, why:"C never accumulates into the toxic zone at that dose and frequency."},
  {t:"A: its doses are the smallest of the three", correct:false, why:"A has the longest half-life and reaches the toxic zone by about the third dose."},
  {t:"B: its half-life equals the dosing interval, so it levels off", correct:false, why:"B accumulates and enters the toxic zone by about the fourth or fifth dose."},
  {t:"All three reach it by the second dose", correct:false, why:"Only A and B reach it, and later than the second dose."},
  {t:"None of them: the doses are quartered", correct:false, why:"A and B accumulate because they are not cleared between doses."}],
 teach:"With repeated quarter doses, a compound accumulates when its half-life is long compared with the dosing interval. A reaches the toxic zone by about the third dose and B by about the fourth or fifth, but C, cleared between doses, never does. For a medication, the dosing frequency is chosen so that the concentration stays below toxicity and, ideally, within the therapeutic range, like C.",
 quote:"How about C? It's got a, it's got a really short half-life. It's clearing really quickly. Does it ever get into the toxic zone after repeated exposures? No, it doesn't, right?",
 note:"In one sentence she said the body clears a compound between spaced exposures \"because the half-life is long\"; her reading of the figure, and the keyed answer, is the reverse: \"C has the shortest half-life. It gets metabolized and cleared really quickly\".",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Exposure: Frequency’; transcript 10/9"},

{id:"L13-016", lecture:"L13", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L13", sub:"freq", skill:"apply", multi:true,
 concept:"adverse-despite-low-concentration", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"Compound C stays below the toxic blood concentration at its dose and frequency. Which of the following could still produce a bad response to it? Select all that apply.",
 options:[
  {t:"An allergic reaction", correct:true, why:"An allergy needs very little drug and has nothing to do with the blood concentration."},
  {t:"Toxicity at a different target than the one the curve describes", correct:true, why:"The curve shows one target; the compound may act elsewhere."},
  {t:"Synergy with another drug, giving additive adverse effects", correct:true, why:"A class suggestion she called great: additive adverse effects with another drug."},
  {t:"Irritation or damage at a subcutaneous or intradermal injection site", correct:true, why:"Local damage at the injection site is an adverse response unrelated to kinetics."},
  {t:"Accumulation of C in the blood over the course of the dosing", correct:false, why:"C does not accumulate; that is the point of its short half-life."}],
 teach:"A compound that is kept below its toxic blood concentration can still cause harm by routes that have nothing to do with kinetics: an allergy, action at a different target, synergy with another drug, or irritation at the injection site. The healthcare provider still has to watch for these.",
 quote:"Could you still have a bad response to it? Yeah ... you could be allergic to it ... Might be going to a different target site ... Could synergize with other drugs ... you inject the drug, you get a therapeutic response to it, but you're causing damage at the injection site",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Exposure: Frequency’; transcript 10/9"},

/* ---------------- ADMET: bioactivation ---------------- */
{id:"L13-017", lecture:"L13", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L13", sub:"admet", skill:"tell",
 concept:"bioactivation-vs-detoxification", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Metabolism that turns a compound into something more harmful than the parent is called:",
 options:[
  {t:"Bioactivation", correct:true, why:"Biotransformation of a potential poison to an active, harmful form; her preferred word is bioactivation."},
  {t:"Detoxification", correct:false, why:"Detoxification is biotransformation that eliminates the toxicant or prevents its formation."},
  {t:"First-pass effect", correct:false, why:"First pass is where metabolism happens after an oral dose, not what the metabolism produces."},
  {t:"Conjugation", correct:false, why:"Conjugation (glucuronide, sulfate) is usually detoxifying."},
  {t:"Elimination", correct:false, why:"Elimination removes the compound; it does not make it more noxious."}],
 teach:"Within ADMET, the metabolism step can go two ways. Bioactivation (toxication, metabolic activation) is biotransformation to an active, harmful form; detoxification is biotransformation that eliminates the ultimate toxicant or prevents it from forming. Acetaminophen can go either way.",
 quote:"I use the word bioactivation for that. Some people use the word toxication, toxification ... when metabolism occurs to create something that's more noxious than what you were originally exposed to.",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘ADME’; transcript 10/9"},

{id:"L13-018", lecture:"L13", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L13", sub:"admet", skill:"apply",
 concept:"acetaminophen-napqi", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"When a dose of acetaminophen exceeds what the phase 2 enzymes can handle, which enzyme produces the liver-toxic metabolite NAPQI?",
 options:[
  {t:"CYP2E1", correct:true, why:"The overflow goes down the P450 pathway, specifically CYP2E1, which forms NAPQI."},
  {t:"A glucuronosyl transferase", correct:false, why:"Glucuronidation is the phase 2 route that detoxifies most of the dose."},
  {t:"A sulfotransferase", correct:false, why:"Sulfation is the other phase 2 route that handles the normal dose."},
  {t:"Monoamine oxidase", correct:false, why:"Monoamine oxidase is not part of acetaminophen metabolism."},
  {t:"Acetylcholinesterase", correct:false, why:"Acetylcholinesterase breaks down acetylcholine, not acetaminophen."}],
 teach:"Most of a normal daily dose of acetaminophen is handled by two families of phase 2 enzymes, the glucuronosyl transferases and the sulfotransferases, giving a glucuronide or a sulfate. When those enzymes cannot keep up, the dose spills into the phase 1 P450 route, CYP2E1, which makes NAPQI, the metabolite that is toxic to the liver. That spill-over is bioactivation.",
 quote:"it goes down through our P450s to do, you know, one of our phase one enzymes, right? Specifically P4452E1. We'll do this work to create that nasty NAQI, OK? And you guys already know that the NAQI metabolite is toxic to the liver",
 note:"On the slide and in speech she abbreviates the glucuronosyl transferases as \"GSTs\" (\"GST's ST's\"); in Katzung that abbreviation usually means glutathione S-transferase. Her meaning, from her words, is the glucuronyl (glucuronosyl) transferases.",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slides ‘ADME: Metabolism’, ‘Example of Bioactivation’; transcript 10/9"},

{id:"L13-019", lecture:"L13", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L13", sub:"admet", skill:"recall",
 concept:"acetaminophen-daily-max", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"The total daily dose of acetaminophen that FDA labeling says not to exceed is:",
 options:[
  {t:"4 g", correct:true, why:"Four grams in 24 hours is the labeled maximum; she advises her older relatives to stay below it."},
  {t:"2 g", correct:false, why:"Lower than the labeled maximum, though she suggests less than 4 g for older adults."},
  {t:"6 g", correct:false, why:"Above the labeled maximum."},
  {t:"8 g", correct:false, why:"Well above the labeled maximum."},
  {t:"500 mg", correct:false, why:"That is one typical tablet, not a daily total."}],
 teach:"The FDA labeling sets 4 g as the acetaminophen total in a 24-hour period. Her advice to older adults is to stay under that, because the phase 2 enzymes that detoxify the drug may be fewer with age, so more of the dose spills into CYP2E1 and NAPQI.",
 quote:"Total daily dose of acetaminophen that we should all be sticking to, not taking more of. 4 g, right? That's on the FDA labeling, right?",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Example of Bioactivation’; transcript 10/9"},

{id:"L13-020", lecture:"L13", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L13", sub:"admet", skill:"apply", multi:true,
 concept:"napqi-below-4g", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"A patient never exceeds 4 g of acetaminophen a day yet develops liver injury. Which of the following could explain NAPQI forming at that dose? Select all that apply.",
 options:[
  {t:"A genetic variation that lowers the activity of the glucuronosyl transferases or sulfotransferases", correct:true, why:"With less phase 2 activity, part of a normal dose is pushed through CYP2E1 to NAPQI; this is pharmacogenomics."},
  {t:"Induction of CYP2E1 by alcohol", correct:true, why:"Alcohol induces CYP2E1, so more of the normal dose becomes NAPQI; her baseball-weekend example."},
  {t:"Inhibition of CYP2E1 by alcohol", correct:false, why:"Alcohol induces, not inhibits, CYP2E1 in her account."},
  {t:"Increased glucuronidation", correct:false, why:"More phase 2 activity would detoxify more of the dose, not less."},
  {t:"Taking the dose at bedtime", correct:false, why:"Timing of the dose was not a factor she named."}],
 teach:"Two things can set up liver toxicity at or below the allowed dose: a genetic variant that weakens the phase 2 enzymes, so some of the dose goes through CYP2E1, and induction of CYP2E1 by alcohol, so more NAPQI is made. Both push a normal dose toward bioactivation.",
 quote:"They may have a genetic variation. Of those enzymes that reduces those phase two enzymes activity ... sometimes you can get an increased amount of 20 being made by the body. We call that induction ... Alcohol can induce 20 expression.",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Example of Bioactivation’; transcript 10/9"},

/* ---------------- Dose ---------------- */
{id:"L13-021", lecture:"L13", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L13", sub:"dose", skill:"recall",
 concept:"dose-makes-poison", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"\"The dose makes the poison\" is attributed to:",
 options:[
  {t:"Paracelsus", correct:true, why:"The saying is attributed to Paracelsus, who studied the toxic effects of compounds long ago."},
  {t:"Casarett and Doull", correct:false, why:"They wrote the toxicology textbook the figures come from."},
  {t:"Hippocrates", correct:false, why:"Not the name she gave."},
  {t:"Katzung", correct:false, why:"Katzung is the pharmacology textbook."},
  {t:"Galen", correct:false, why:"Not the name she gave."}],
 teach:"Dose is a principle of toxicology because the same compound can be harmless or lethal depending on how much reaches the target. The saying that the dose makes the poison is attributed to Paracelsus.",
 quote:"Dose makes the poison. Who said that? Anybody know who said that? Supposed to be attributed to a guy named Paracelsus",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Dose’; transcript 10/9"},

{id:"L13-022", lecture:"L13", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L13", sub:"dose", skill:"recall",
 concept:"ld50-definition", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"The LD50 of a compound is the dose that:",
 options:[
  {t:"Kills 50% of the animals given it", correct:true, why:"The lethal dose for half of the exposed population, measured in rats for the table she showed."},
  {t:"Produces the desired effect in 50% of animals", correct:false, why:"That is the ED50, the effective dose."},
  {t:"Produces a toxic but non-lethal effect in 50% of animals", correct:false, why:"That is the TD50, the toxic dose."},
  {t:"Is the lowest dose that causes any effect", correct:false, why:"That is the threshold dose."},
  {t:"Is half of the lethal dose", correct:false, why:"LD50 is a dose that is lethal to half the population, not half of some other dose."}],
 teach:"LD50 is the lethal dose for 50% of the exposed animals. It is read from a population dose–response curve at 50% cumulative mortality. The range across compounds is enormous.",
 quote:"this is the lethal dose that will kill off 50% of the animals given this compound",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Dose’; transcript 10/9"},

{id:"L13-023", lecture:"L13", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L13", sub:"dose", skill:"apply",
 concept:"ld50-table-range", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"In the rat LD50 table, which compound needs the smallest dose to kill half the animals?",
 options:[
  {t:"Botulinum toxin, LD50 0.00001 mg/kg", correct:true, why:"The bottom of the table and the most potent poison listed."},
  {t:"Ethyl alcohol, LD50 10,000 mg/kg", correct:false, why:"The top of the table: the largest dose is needed, so it is the least potent poison listed."},
  {t:"Nicotine, LD50 1 mg/kg", correct:false, why:"Potent, but botulinum toxin is 100,000 times more so."},
  {t:"Dioxin (TCDD), LD50 0.001 mg/kg", correct:false, why:"Second from the bottom; botulinum toxin is 100 times more potent."},
  {t:"Phenobarbital sodium, LD50 150 mg/kg", correct:false, why:"Near the top of the table: a large dose is needed."}],
 teach:"The table adapted from Casarett and Doull lists rat LD50 values from ethyl alcohol at 10,000 mg/kg down through sodium chloride 4,000, morphine sulfate 900, phenobarbital sodium 150, strychnine sulfate 2, nicotine 1, tetrodotoxin 0.1, dioxin 0.001 and botulinum toxin 0.00001 mg/kg. The dose makes the poison, but the compound sets the scale: the range spans nine orders of magnitude.",
 quote:"10,000 mgs per kilogram for ethyl alcohol would be the LD50 in rats, and 0.00001 would be the LD50 for botulinum toxin in rats, OK? So the dose makes the poison, but you have to know what compound you're talking about too.",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Dose’ (Casarett & Doull Table 2-1); transcript 10/9"},

{id:"L13-024", lecture:"L13", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L13", sub:"dose", skill:"apply",
 concept:"botulinum-can", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript", lowYield:true,
 stem:"A can of corn squirts when it is opened. Why should it be thrown away rather than cooked?",
 options:[
  {t:"Botulinum toxin may be in the can, and cooking does not destroy it", correct:true, why:"The gas from the multiplying bacteria is what makes the can squirt; the toxin, not the bacteria, is lethal, and heating does not break it down."},
  {t:"The corn has fermented into alcohol, and cooking would concentrate it", correct:false, why:"Fermentation is not the hazard she described."},
  {t:"The can has rusted and leaked iron", correct:false, why:"Iron is not the hazard; botulinum toxin is."},
  {t:"Cooking would concentrate the toxin", correct:false, why:"Cooking neither concentrates nor destroys it; the point is that it does not destroy it."},
  {t:"Acidic foods like tomatoes carry the same risk", correct:false, why:"She said acidic foods such as tomatoes are usually fine; the risk is in low-acid vegetables such as beans, peas and corn."}],
 teach:"Her public-service note on the LD50 table: botulinum bacteria can grow inside a sealed can of low-acid vegetables (not usually tomatoes) because they do not need oxygen; as they multiply they release the toxin and gas, so the can squirts on opening. Cooking does not break down botulinum toxin, so the can is thrown away.",
 quote:"it's not the bacteria that, that kills you, it's the botulinum toxin that those bacteria are making as they're growing in your can of corn, OK? ... you can't heat it, cook it, and break down that botulinum toxin",
 note:"She called the organism \"an aerobic bacteria\" and then said \"it doesn't need oxygen\"; the second statement is the one that matches botulinum growth in a sealed can. Marked low yield: a public-service aside, not an objective.",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Dose’; transcript 10/9"},

/* ---------------- Individual vs population dose–response ---------------- */
{id:"L13-025", lecture:"L13", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L13", sub:"curves", skill:"tell",
 concept:"individual-vs-population-curve", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Groups of rodents receive 0, 2.5, 5 and higher mg/kg doses of an organophosphate, and the percent inhibition of carboxylesterase and of cholinesterase is plotted against dose. This is a(n):",
 options:[
  {t:"Individual dose–response relationship", correct:true, why:"The response of individuals to graded doses; here two enzymes are inhibited, two targets."},
  {t:"Population dose–response relationship (cumulative mortality)", correct:false, why:"A population curve groups responses (such as deaths) by dose; it gives LD50 and threshold, not enzyme inhibition per individual."},
  {t:"Quantal curve of grouped animals", correct:false, why:"Grouping does not make it quantal; each animal's graded enzyme inhibition is measured."},
  {t:"Hormetic curve", correct:false, why:"Nothing beneficial is measured."},
  {t:"Cumulative mortality curve", correct:false, why:"No deaths are counted."}],
 teach:"An individual dose–response describes the response of an individual to varying doses (here, graded enzyme inhibition at each dose, sometimes on a log dose axis). Measuring the right things on such a curve reveals that many chemicals have more than one effect because of multiple targets: the organophosphate inhibits both carboxylesterase and cholinesterase. A population dose–response describes how a population responds to varying doses and yields LD50 and threshold.",
 quote:"many chemicals have more than one effect because they have multiple targets. For example, these organophosphate exposures are inhibiting these two different kinds of enzymes. And we could not see this if we weren't graphing it as an individual dose response curve",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘First Dose-Response Relationship’ (C&D Fig 2-5); transcript 10/9"},

{id:"L13-026", lecture:"L13", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L13", sub:"curves", skill:"figure",
 concept:"population-curve-ld50-threshold", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"On a cumulative mortality curve from a population dose–response study, the LD50 and the threshold dose are read at:",
 options:[
  {t:"LD50 at 50% mortality; threshold at the lowest dose with any effect", correct:true, why:"In the figure she read, 50% cumulative mortality fell at about 100 mg/kg and the first effect at about 10 mg/kg."},
  {t:"LD50 at the highest dose given; threshold at 50% cumulative mortality", correct:false, why:"This swaps the two readings."},
  {t:"Both at the peak of the mortality-frequency histogram", correct:false, why:"The peak of the frequency plot is the most common lethal dose, not the threshold."},
  {t:"LD50 at 100% mortality; threshold at 0%", correct:false, why:"LD50 is defined at 50%, and threshold is the first dose with an effect, not zero effect."},
  {t:"They cannot be read from a population curve", correct:false, why:"Finding LD50 and threshold is exactly what population curves are for."}],
 teach:"A population dose–response is plotted as mortality frequency against dose and, more usefully, as cumulative mortality. The LD50 is the dose at 50% cumulative mortality; the threshold is the lowest dose at which an effect first appears. The probit-unit version in the textbook figure is old-fashioned and, in her words, nobody uses it.",
 quote:"this is where 50% of the animals die, and the dose here is about 100 mg per kg. OK. And then the threshold would be showing you. Where is the dose where you first start seeing an effect happening",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Second Dose-Response Relationship’ (C&D Fig 2-6); transcript 10/9"},

/* ---------------- Shapes and threshold ---------------- */
{id:"L13-027", lecture:"L13", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L13", sub:"shape", skill:"tell",
 concept:"u-shape-essential-vs-hormesis", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Iron and red wine both give U-shaped dose–response curves. What separates the two curves?",
 options:[
  {t:"Only the nutrient curve has a region of homeostasis", correct:true, why:"Iron: deficiency (death) → homeostasis → overdose. Red wine: a possible protective effect at a low dose, then an adverse effect, with no region of need."},
  {t:"Only the hormetic curve has a region of homeostasis between its arms", correct:false, why:"This is reversed."},
  {t:"Only the nutrient curve is U-shaped", correct:false, why:"Both are U-shaped; the figure shows two different reasons for the shape."},
  {t:"The hormetic curve rises with dose from the start", correct:false, why:"A hormetic curve first falls (benefit) and then rises (harm)."},
  {t:"The nutrient curve has no toxic region", correct:false, why:"Iron can be overdosed; the right arm of the U is toxicity."}],
 teach:"Two U-shaped curves that look alike. For an essential substance such as iron, vitamin A or vitamin D, too little is harmful (deficiency), the middle is the region of homeostasis, and too much is toxic. For a non-essential substance such as red wine, hormesis means a low dose may be protective and a higher dose adverse, with no region of homeostasis. Which one applies depends on what the exposure is.",
 quote:"because this is not an essential nutrient, an essential element that we need, there's really no area of homeostasis ... it's illustrating the example of Homesis",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Shape of Dose-Response’ (C&D Fig 2-7); transcript 10/9"},

{id:"L13-028", lecture:"L13", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L13", sub:"shape", skill:"recall",
 concept:"hormesis", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Hormesis describes a substance that:",
 options:[
  {t:"Helps at a low dose and harms at a higher dose, without being essential", correct:true, why:"Her example is a glass or a glass and a half of red wine as possibly heart protective, then too much."},
  {t:"Is essential at a low dose and lethal when absent from the diet entirely", correct:false, why:"That is the essential-nutrient U-curve (deficiency and homeostasis), not hormesis."},
  {t:"Has no threshold", correct:false, why:"A no-threshold linear response describes carcinogens."},
  {t:"Accumulates with repeated dosing", correct:false, why:"Accumulation is the frequency and half-life discussion."},
  {t:"Is more toxic by the oral route", correct:false, why:"Route is a separate principle."}],
 teach:"Hormesis is the U-shaped response of a non-essential substance: a low dose produces a beneficial or protective effect and a higher dose an adverse one. Because the substance is not needed, there is no region of homeostasis. It is a concept toxicologists use; few others do.",
 quote:"my glass or glass and half of red wine is possibly protective, and then I go too far and I should not be driving. OK? So, again, this is a U-shaped curve.",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Shape of Dose-Response’; transcript 10/9"},

{id:"L13-029", lecture:"L13", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L13", sub:"shape", skill:"tell",
 concept:"threshold-vs-linear", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"For which kind of agent is a linear, no-threshold dose–response (a response possible from the first molecule) used as the model?",
 options:[
  {t:"Carcinogens", correct:true, why:"That is how carcinogens were long modeled: linear from zero, possibly no safe dose."},
  {t:"Medications", correct:false, why:"Medications follow the S-shaped curve with a threshold below which there is no effect."},
  {t:"Essential nutrients", correct:false, why:"Essential nutrients give the U-shaped curve with a region of homeostasis."},
  {t:"Most poisons", correct:false, why:"Most poisons, like medications, have a dose below which nothing happens."},
  {t:"Hormetic substances", correct:false, why:"Hormesis is U-shaped, not linear."}],
 teach:"An S-shaped dose–response implies a threshold: a dose below which there is no effect, therapeutic or toxic. That is probably the case for medications and most poisons. Carcinogens have been modeled as linear with no threshold, on the theory that a single molecule damaging DNA could start a cancer. Her qualifier: possibly.",
 quote:"That's a theory for exposure to carcinogens. Maybe it just takes one molecule of a carcinogen to cause you problems ... So don't always assume everything has an area where a dose is not harmful.",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Threshold Dose’; transcript 10/9"},

{id:"L13-030", lecture:"L13", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L13", sub:"shape", skill:"apply",
 concept:"cumulative-dose-threshold", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"In the hamster study of 2-acetylaminofluorene (2-AAF), the dose at which bladder tumors first appeared was lower in animals sacrificed at 33 months than in animals sacrificed at 24 months. Why did the threshold fall?",
 options:[
  {t:"Their cumulative dose was higher, so a lower daily dose crossed the threshold", correct:true, why:"Cumulative dose is the keyword a student gave and she confirmed."},
  {t:"The older animals metabolized the compound faster", correct:false, why:"Metabolism was not the explanation; the total dose received was."},
  {t:"The compound became more potent with storage", correct:false, why:"Nothing about the compound changed."},
  {t:"The 33-month animals were given a higher daily dose than the 24-month animals", correct:false, why:"The daily doses were the same groups; only the duration differed."},
  {t:"Bladder tumors occur spontaneously in old hamsters", correct:false, why:"That was true of liver tumors, not bladder tumors, in this study."}],
 teach:"With daily dosing, a longer exposure means a higher cumulative dose, so the threshold for a response falls when animals are observed longer. In the 2-AAF study the bladder-tumor threshold was about 75 ppm at 24 months and about 45 ppm at 33 months. She said she would not put a graph like this on an exam; the point is what cumulative dose does to a threshold.",
 quote:"So the cumulative dose that these animals got was higher at 33 months, right, compared to 24 months ... That's why it didn't take as much of the dose to cause threshold.",
 note:"\"I will not give you a graph like that I never have ... It's very unlikely I would give you this kind of graph on an exam.\"",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘More on Threshold Dose’ (C&D Fig 2-8); transcript 10/9"},

{id:"L13-031", lecture:"L13", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L13", sub:"shape", skill:"apply",
 concept:"cancer-disease-of-aging", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"In the same 2-AAF study, hamsters that received no compound at all and were sacrificed at 33 months had liver tumors. What does this show?",
 options:[
  {t:"Cancer is a disease of aging; old hamsters are a poor model for liver tumors", correct:true, why:"The zero-dose, 33-month group had a significant number of liver tumors on their own."},
  {t:"The control animals were dosed by mistake for the final nine months of the study", correct:false, why:"It was a well-done study; the controls truly received no 2-AAF."},
  {t:"2-AAF causes liver tumors at any dose", correct:false, why:"The tumors in the zero-dose group cannot be caused by a compound they never received."},
  {t:"Bladder tumors spread to the liver", correct:false, why:"Nothing in the study pointed to spread."},
  {t:"The liver tumors were caused by the diet", correct:false, why:"Age, not diet, was the explanation."}],
 teach:"The second lesson from the 2-AAF figure: the untreated 33-month hamsters had liver tumors, because cancer is a disease of aging and old hamsters develop liver tumors anyway. A species that gets the tumor spontaneously is the wrong model for testing whether a compound causes that tumor.",
 quote:"Is cancer the disease of the aged? Yes, cancer is a disease of the aged, usually ... if we're gonna look and see if 2AAF causes liver cancer, hamsters are not the best animals to use for this, because they get them anyway",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘More on Threshold Dose’; transcript 10/9"},

/* ---------------- Indices and target (slides she did not reach on 10/9) ---------------- */
{id:"L13-032", lecture:"L13", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L13", sub:"indices", skill:"recall",
 concept:"ti-mos-moe", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Which index of toxicity gives information about the slope of the dose–response curves, and how is it calculated?",
 options:[
  {t:"Margin of safety, MOS = TD1 / ED99", correct:true, why:"Comparing the dose toxic to 1% with the dose effective in 99% depends on how steep the two curves are."},
  {t:"Therapeutic index, TI = toxic dose / therapeutic dose", correct:false, why:"The therapeutic index provides no information about the slope."},
  {t:"Margin of exposure, MOE = NOAEL / exposure", correct:false, why:"The margin of exposure is for repeated exposures to non-therapeutic compounds, not for slope."},
  {t:"LD50 / ED50", correct:false, why:"That ratio is a therapeutic index, which says nothing about slope."},
  {t:"ED99 / TD1", correct:false, why:"The margin of safety divides the toxic dose by the effective dose, not the reverse."}],
 teach:"Three values come from dose–response curves. The therapeutic index compares a toxic dose with a therapeutic dose (TI = toxic dose / therapeutic dose) and gives no information about slope. The margin of safety, MOS = TD1 / ED99, does carry slope information. Both are for single doses. The margin of exposure, MOE = NOAEL / exposure, is for repeated exposures to non-therapeutic compounds.",
 quote:"most of the time, a margin of safety number is developed because we say what's the toxic dose for 1% of the animals over the effective dose of 99% of the animals. That's the margin of safety. And because we use a different percentage, it gives you an idea of the slope of your dose response curve, whereas the therapeutic index does not.",
 note:"Taught 10/13. Her rule for the therapeutic index: the percentage need not be 50, but the numerator and the denominator must use the same percentage; the margin of safety uses two different percentages, which is why it carries slope information.",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Values Developed from Dose-Response’; transcript 10/13"},

{id:"L13-033", lecture:"L13", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L13", sub:"indices", skill:"recall",
 concept:"moe-repeated", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"The margin of exposure (MOE) is used for:",
 options:[
  {t:"Repeated exposures to non-therapeutic compounds", correct:true, why:"The slide marks MOE for repeated exposures and TI and MOS for single doses."},
  {t:"Single doses of a medication, compared with its toxic dose", correct:false, why:"Single doses use the therapeutic index or the margin of safety."},
  {t:"Comparing two drugs' potencies", correct:false, why:"Potency comparison reads ED50 values, not MOE."},
  {t:"Finding the LD50", correct:false, why:"LD50 comes from the population curve."},
  {t:"Defining the threshold dose", correct:false, why:"The threshold is read from the curve; the NOAEL is the related no-effect level used in MOE."}],
 teach:"The margin of exposure divides the NOAEL (no observed adverse effect level) by the actual exposure; it is the index for repeated exposures to compounds that are not medicines, such as pollutants.",
 quote:"the margin of exposure, this is something that we use truly in toxicology with exposures to something that's not therapeutic, like to, you know, a pollutant, an occupational exposure, OK, and it's also for repeated exposures",
 note:"Taught 10/13. She says NOAEL as 'no observable adverse effect level' (the slide prints 'no observed') and described it as the dose given to the animals at which they had no bad response; she called it the lowest such dose.",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Values Developed from Dose-Response’; transcript 10/13"},

{id:"L13-034", lecture:"L13", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L13", sub:"indices", skill:"recall",
 concept:"target-not-highest-concentration", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Which statement about the target of a toxic substance is correct?",
 options:[
  {t:"The target organ may not be where the concentration is highest", correct:true, why:"Both lines are on the target-and-mechanism slide."},
  {t:"The target organ is always where the concentration is highest", correct:false, why:"The slide says the opposite."},
  {t:"A substance has only one target", correct:false, why:"The slide lists multiple targets with variation in toxicity among them."},
  {t:"The mechanism is the same at every target", correct:false, why:"The mechanism of action at multiple targets may differ."},
  {t:"The dose given, not the dose at the target, determines toxicity", correct:false, why:"The dose at the target determines toxicity."}],
 teach:"Target and mechanism of action: the target can be an organ or a physiological endpoint; there may be multiple targets with different toxicity and different mechanisms at each; the target organ may not be the site of highest concentration; and the dose at the target is what determines toxicity.",
 quote:"where it's having an adverse effect may not be where the highest concentration is, which is probably a little counterintuitive, right?",
 note:"Taught 10/13; her words match the slide. Her example: the kidney may be more sensitive than muscle at a lower concentration, and there is still a dose–response curve at the kidney itself.",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Target & Mechanism of Action’; transcript 10/13"},

/* ---------------- The reading ---------------- */
{id:"L13-035", dupOf:"L13-043", lecture:"L13", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L13", sub:"reading", skill:"apply", readingId:"rodriguez-mozaz", lowYield:true,
 concept:"reading-source-wwtp", tags:["smith","reading"], source:"slide",
 stem:"Applying the source principle to pharmaceuticals in water: which point is most often identified as the main discharge of pharmaceuticals into surface waters?",
 options:[
  {t:"Wastewater treatment plant effluents", correct:true, why:"Current treatment does not target pharmaceuticals for removal, so the plants are the main points of discharge."},
  {t:"Pharmaceutical factories", correct:false, why:"Manufacturing is one source, but the review names treatment-plant effluent as the main discharge point."},
  {t:"Rainfall", correct:false, why:"Rain is not a source of pharmaceuticals."},
  {t:"Drinking-water treatment plant intakes", correct:false, why:"Those plants are where the trace levels are found, not where they enter."},
  {t:"Hospitals only", correct:false, why:"Human and veterinary use throughout a community feeds the sewage that reaches the plants."}],
 teach:"The assigned review on pharmaceuticals in water carries the five principles. Source: unmetabolized drug and metabolites from human and veterinary use enter waste streams, and wastewater treatment plants, which do not specifically remove pharmaceuticals, are the main discharge points into surface water; unused medicine that is flushed adds to it.",
 note:"On 10/13 she named treatment plants as one of four sources (\"the current mechanisms don't get all the drugs out of it before it goes back into your streams and rivers and lakes\") and said the water is a source for both humans and animals. The 'main discharge' ranking is the article's, not hers, so the item stays low yield; L14-024 tests her point.",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Discussion of Reading Assignment’; RodriguezMozaz-EHP-2010.pdf p. 1017"},

{id:"L13-036", lecture:"L13", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L13", sub:"reading", skill:"apply", readingId:"rodriguez-mozaz", lowYield:true,
 concept:"reading-wildlife-effects", tags:["smith","reading"], source:"slide",
 stem:"Which organisms were adversely affected by pharmaceuticals in the environment in the two examples the reading calls extraordinary?",
 options:[
  {t:"Vultures (diclofenac) and fathead minnows (ethinyl estradiol)", correct:true, why:"The vulture decline in India and Pakistan and the fathead minnow collapse are the two examples."},
  {t:"Humans drinking treated water", correct:false, why:"The review found little evidence of human harm at part-per-trillion levels."},
  {t:"Cattle treated with antibiotics", correct:false, why:"The cattle were the source of the diclofenac, not the affected organisms."},
  {t:"Bacteria in sediments", correct:false, why:"Antibiotic resistance in sediments is raised as a possible effect, not one of the two examples."},
  {t:"Farm crops irrigated with reclaimed water from treatment plant effluent", correct:false, why:"Crops are not among the affected organisms described."}],
 teach:"Target and dose in the reading: vultures feeding on carcasses of diclofenac-treated cattle died of renal failure because they could not excrete the drug, and fathead minnows in a lake deliberately dosed with low levels of ethinyl estradiol showed cellular and tissue responses, then a population collapse by the second year. Humans, exposed at part-per-trillion levels in drinking water, receive far less than a therapeutic dose over a lifetime, so ecosystems appear more at risk than people.",
 note:"Not discussed in class: on 10/13 she did not mention the vultures, diclofenac, fathead minnows or ethinyl estradiol. Her wildlife example was frogs, fish and alligators turning more female where estrogen levels are higher (L14-030). Kept low yield; the article text keys it.",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Discussion of Reading Assignment’; RodriguezMozaz-EHP-2010.pdf pp. 1017–1018"},

/* ---------------- Her practice quiz 1 (verbatim, with her keys); shown on the "Quizzes (verbatim)" card (QZ3, sub quiz1) ---------------- */
{id:"L13-037", lecture:"QZ3", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"QZ3", sub:"quiz1", skill:"apply",
 concept:"adverse-despite-low-concentration", tags:["smith","quiz","verbatim"], source:"slide",
 stem:"A compound with a half-life that allows it to be cleared from the body before the next exposure may still result in chronic adverse effects",
 options:[
  {t:"True", correct:true, why:"Her key. Compound C on the frequency figure never reaches the toxic blood concentration, yet she listed allergy, another target, synergy with other drugs and injection-site damage as ways it can still harm."},
  {t:"False", correct:false, why:"Her key is True: clearance between doses rules out accumulation, not every adverse response."}],
 teach:"Practice quiz 1, question 1. The point is her twist on compound C: an exposure that never accumulates can still cause an adverse response by a route that has nothing to do with kinetics.",
 note:"Verbatim from her practice quiz; the key is the one she released. Same point as L13-016.",
 cite:"Smith_Practice_Quiz_1_-_Principles_of_Toxicology.pdf question 1"},

{id:"L13-038", lecture:"QZ3", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"QZ3", sub:"quiz1", skill:"recall",
 concept:"exposure-durations", tags:["smith","quiz","verbatim"], source:"slide",
 stem:"Chronic exposure is considered to be repeated exposure for what length of time?",
 options:[
  {t:"Less than 24 hours", correct:false, why:"Acute."},
  {t:"1-3 months", correct:false, why:"Subchronic."},
  {t:"More than three months", correct:true, why:"Her key: chronic is more than 3 months, up to years."},
  {t:"Less than a month", correct:false, why:"Subacute."}],
 teach:"Practice quiz 1, question 2: the animal-study durations. Acute less than 24 hours; subacute less than 1 month; subchronic 1 to 3 months; chronic more than 3 months.",
 note:"Verbatim from her practice quiz with her key. Same table as L13-010.",
 cite:"Smith_Practice_Quiz_1_-_Principles_of_Toxicology.pdf question 2"},

{id:"L13-039", lecture:"QZ3", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"QZ3", sub:"quiz1", skill:"recall",
 concept:"ti-mos-moe", tags:["smith","quiz","verbatim"], source:"slide",
 stem:"What value derived from a dose-response curve has the following formula: X = Toxic Dose/Therapeutic Dose",
 options:[
  {t:"Threshold Dose", correct:false, why:"The threshold is read from the curve, not calculated as a ratio."},
  {t:"Margin of Safety", correct:false, why:"MOS = TD1 / ED99: two different percentages."},
  {t:"Margin of Exposure", correct:false, why:"MOE = NOAEL / exposure."},
  {t:"Therapeutic Index", correct:true, why:"Her key: TI = toxic dose / therapeutic dose, with the same percentage in numerator and denominator."}],
 teach:"Practice quiz 1, question 3. The therapeutic index is the toxic dose over the therapeutic dose at the same percentage of the population; it carries no slope information, which is what separates it from the margin of safety.",
 note:"Verbatim from her practice quiz with her key. Same point as L13-032.",
 cite:"Smith_Practice_Quiz_1_-_Principles_of_Toxicology.pdf question 3"},

{id:"L13-040", lecture:"QZ3", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"QZ3", sub:"quiz1", skill:"recall",
 concept:"admet-t", tags:["smith","quiz","verbatim"], source:"slide",
 stem:"What does ADME stand for?",
 options:[
  {t:"Absorption, Distribution, Metabolism, Excretion", correct:true, why:"Her key, written out on the quiz as a short answer."},
  {t:"Administration, Distribution, Metabolism, Elimination", correct:false, why:"The A is absorption, not administration."},
  {t:"Absorption, Dose, Mechanism, Exposure", correct:false, why:"Dose, mechanism and exposure are other principles, not the letters of ADME."},
  {t:"Absorption, Distribution, Mechanism, Excretion", correct:false, why:"The M is metabolism."}],
 teach:"Practice quiz 1, question 4, a short-answer item on the quiz: absorption, distribution, metabolism, excretion; toxicology adds the T for toxicity (ADMET).",
 note:"On the quiz this was a free-response question, not multiple choice; the exam may ask for the expansion written out. Options added here for the drill.",
 cite:"Smith_Practice_Quiz_1_-_Principles_of_Toxicology.pdf question 4"},

{id:"L13-041", lecture:"QZ3", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"QZ3", sub:"quiz1", skill:"apply",
 concept:"toxic-target-differs", tags:["smith","quiz","verbatim"], source:"slide",
 stem:"The mechanisms of a drug’s toxicity is always the same as the mechanism of its efficacy.",
 options:[
  {t:"True", correct:false, why:"Her key is False: the toxic target can differ from the therapeutic target."},
  {t:"False", correct:true, why:"Her key. The target of something toxic is not always the same as its therapeutic target."}],
 teach:"Practice quiz 1, question 5: the target-and-mechanism principle. A medication's toxicity may act at a different target, by a different mechanism, from its therapeutic effect.",
 note:"Verbatim from her practice quiz with her key. Same point as L13-005.",
 cite:"Smith_Practice_Quiz_1_-_Principles_of_Toxicology.pdf question 5"},

{id:"L13-042", lecture:"QZ3", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"QZ3", sub:"quiz1", skill:"apply",
 concept:"target-not-highest-concentration", tags:["smith","quiz","verbatim"], source:"slide",
 stem:"The concentration of a medication in the body is always highest at its target site.",
 options:[
  {t:"True", correct:false, why:"Her key is False."},
  {t:"False", correct:true, why:"Her key. The target organ may not be the site of highest concentration; the dose at the target determines toxicity."}],
 teach:"Practice quiz 1, question 6: the target-and-mechanism slide she covered on 10/13. Where a compound has its adverse effect may not be where its concentration is highest; the kidney can be more sensitive than muscle at a lower concentration.",
 note:"Verbatim from her practice quiz with her key. Same point as L13-034.",
 cite:"Smith_Practice_Quiz_1_-_Principles_of_Toxicology.pdf question 6"},

{id:"L13-043", readingId:"rodriguez-mozaz", lecture:"QZ3", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"QZ3", sub:"quiz1", skill:"apply",
 concept:"reading-source-human-use", tags:["smith","quiz","verbatim"], source:"slide",
 stem:"The article by Rodriguez-Mozaz discusses the fact that pharmaceuticals have been detected in ground water, surface water, and drinking water. What is most likely the greatest source of pharmaceuticals in those waters?",
 options:[
  {t:"Unused medications thrown in the trash leaching out of landfills.", correct:false, why:"A source, but not the greatest."},
  {t:"Pharmaceutical company manufacturing processes.", correct:false, why:"A source, but not the greatest."},
  {t:"Patients flushing unused medications down the toilet.", correct:false, why:"A source she discussed, but not the greatest."},
  {t:"Consumption of medications by humans and the resulting waste streams", correct:true, why:"Her key: the drug and its metabolites that people excrete after normal use reach the waste stream and the treatment plants."}],
 teach:"Practice quiz 1, question 7: the source principle applied to the article. The greatest source is ordinary human use, excreted into the waste stream; flushing, trash and manufacturing are smaller contributors.",
 note:"Verbatim from her practice quiz with her key. L13-035 and L14-024 test the same source principle; this wording is hers.",
 cite:"Smith_Practice_Quiz_1_-_Principles_of_Toxicology.pdf question 7"},

{id:"L13-044", readingId:"rodriguez-mozaz", lecture:"QZ3", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"QZ3", sub:"quiz1", skill:"apply",
 concept:"reading-human-dose-below-therapeutic", tags:["smith","quiz","verbatim"], source:"slide",
 stem:"The dose of pharmaceuticals that people are exposed to in drinking water exceeds the daily dose of that medication when used therapeutically.",
 options:[
  {t:"True", correct:false, why:"Her key is False."},
  {t:"False", correct:true, why:"Her key. Drinking-water levels are far below a therapeutic daily dose."}],
 teach:"Practice quiz 1, question 8: the dose principle applied to the article. Human exposure through drinking water is far below a therapeutic dose; the wildlife, living in the water, is the population at risk.",
 note:"Verbatim from her practice quiz with her key.",
 cite:"Smith_Practice_Quiz_1_-_Principles_of_Toxicology.pdf question 8"},

{id:"L13-045", readingId:"rodriguez-mozaz", lecture:"QZ3", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"QZ3", sub:"quiz1", skill:"apply",
 concept:"reading-conclusion", tags:["smith","quiz","verbatim"], source:"slide",
 stem:"Which is the best statement that describes the conclusion the authors made in this article?",
 options:[
  {t:"Pharmaceuticals in water are not adversely affecting aquatic wildlife.", correct:false, why:"The article's wildlife examples show adverse effects."},
  {t:"Pharmaceuticals in waters are adversely affecting human health.", correct:false, why:"The authors did not conclude that; human exposure is far below therapeutic doses."},
  {t:"No answers are correct", correct:false, why:"One answer is correct."},
  {t:"More data is needed to determine if pharmaceuticals in water are adversely affecting human health.", correct:true, why:"Her key: the authors call for more data on human health effects."}],
 teach:"Practice quiz 1, question 9: the authors' conclusion. Wildlife effects are documented; whether trace levels harm human health is not settled, and more data are needed.",
 note:"Verbatim from her practice quiz with her key.",
 cite:"Smith_Practice_Quiz_1_-_Principles_of_Toxicology.pdf question 9"},

{id:"L13-046", lecture:"QZ3", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"QZ3", sub:"quiz1", skill:"recall",
 concept:"five-principles", tags:["smith","quiz","verbatim"], source:"slide",
 stem:"List the five principles of toxicology:",
 options:[
  {t:"Source; Exposure; ADMET; Dose; Target and Mechanism of Action", correct:true, why:"Her key, written out on the quiz as a short answer."},
  {t:"Source; Route; Metabolism; Dose; Receptor", correct:false, why:"Route is part of exposure and metabolism part of ADMET; receptor is not the principle's name."},
  {t:"Toxin; Toxicant; Xenobiotic; Poison; Dose", correct:false, why:"Those are the definitions, not the principles."},
  {t:"Source; Exposure; Half-life; LD50; Threshold", correct:false, why:"Half-life, LD50 and threshold are values inside the exposure and dose principles."}],
 teach:"Practice quiz 1, question 10, a short-answer item on the quiz: source, exposure, ADMET, dose, and target and mechanism of action.",
 note:"On the quiz this was a free-response list; the exam may ask for it written out. Options added here for the drill.",
 cite:"Smith_Practice_Quiz_1_-_Principles_of_Toxicology.pdf question 10"},
/* ---------------- The coral snake on 'What is Toxic?' (her spoken question, 10/9) ---------------- */
{id:"L13-047", lecture:"L13", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L13", sub:"defs", skill:"apply",
 concept:"coral-snake-rhyme", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"A snake has red, yellow and black bands, and its red bands touch its yellow bands. Using the rhyme from class, what is it most likely to be?",
 options:[
  {t:"A venomous coral snake", correct:true, why:"\"Red and yellow kills a fellow\": red touching yellow marks the coral snake."},
  {t:"A harmless king snake", correct:false, why:"On the king snake the red touches the black: \"Red and black, friend of Jack.\""},
  {t:"Either snake; the bands cannot tell them apart", correct:false, why:"The order of the bands is exactly what tells them apart; the two snakes carry the same three colours."},
  {t:"A venomous snake only if it also has black bands", correct:false, why:"Both snakes have black bands; what matters is which colour the red touches."}],
 teach:"The coral snake and the king snake both carry red, yellow and black bands, in a different order. On the coral snake the red touches the yellow (\"Red and yellow kills a fellow\"); on the king snake the red touches the black (\"Red and black, friend of Jack\"), and it is not poisonous: people keep king snakes as pets. The coral snake is very venomous; its venom carries a neurotoxin. It lives in Texas, especially East Texas.",
 quote:"Coral snake, how do you know that? Red and y'all, everybody know that poem. Red and yellow kills a fellow, so if the red touches yellow, it's poisonous, OK? Red and black, friend of Jack.",
 note:"She showed the snakes as a fun opener (\"I just like to know about poisonous animals and plants. I think it's fun.\") before turning to medications; on 10/19 she said everything said in class is testable.",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘What is Toxic?’; transcript 10/9"},
{id:"L13-048", lecture:"L13", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L13", sub:"defs", skill:"recall",
 concept:"coral-snake-venom", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript", dupOf:"L13-047",
 stem:"What kind of toxin does the coral snake's venom carry?",
 options:[
  {t:"A neurotoxin", correct:true, why:"Her words: \"So this is a coral snake, very venomous, they have a neurotoxin.\""},
  {t:"A hepatotoxin", correct:false, why:"She named a neurotoxin for the coral snake, not a liver toxin."},
  {t:"A carcinogen", correct:false, why:"A snakebite is a single, immediate exposure; she named a neurotoxin."},
  {t:"A toxicant", correct:false, why:"Venom is made by the snake, so it is a toxin; a toxicant comes from human activity."}],
 teach:"The coral snake is very venomous; its venom carries a neurotoxin. Being made by the snake, the venom is a toxin, not a toxicant. The look-alike king snake, with red touching black, is not poisonous.",
 quote:"So this is a coral snake, very venomous, they have a neurotoxin. They are here in Texas, um, they're in East Texas, especially",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘What is Toxic?’; transcript 10/9"}
);
